## How it went
The era gate is the whole E7 mechanic and it latched at **t = 14.77 s**: the first 25-gold `sentry_beacon` stood at (23, 37) inside `relay-site-r3`, `PLAYBOOK_USE "relay-valley-1"` installed my own demonstration, and `syncProgramRelays` lit the site on the next step — `relaysLitByProgram: ["relay-site-r3"]`, `uses: 1`, `repeats: 0`, zero refusals.

The decisive move was economic, not mechanical. `lossStakes: []` plus `MOVE_HERO` means the claim is not a loss condition, so I walked the hero off it into the relay site and forted around it — the same beacon that lit the objective was also the fort's first gun. The hero lost **eight hit points in the entire run** (min 98.0 %, finishing 467/475), six works stood with **0 ever wrecked**, and `goldStolen` was **0** with up to 33 thieves alive, because I declined the `stockpile` on a thief roster.

## One honest caveat on the score
**200 is the live bank cap of the plan I chose, not the map's ceiling.** Two stockpiles would raise it to 500; I declined them because a standing till is what makes `nearestGoldHolding` non-empty (a prior generation measured 435 gold stolen that way). The purse hit 200 at t = 424 and `goldPanned` froze at 600 for the closing 176 s. The untested refinement, named in both the outcome file and the report: **buy the pair late (~t = 480)** — theft integrates over the time the till stands, so late exposure is ~120 s against +300 of ceiling.

`gauntlet-outcome.json` and `gauntlet-report.md` are current; the report ends with the four sections in the required order. All files are new and confined to my workspace — no tracked file was touched.
